in this lesson we're going to talk about  percent of change so how do you  calculate percent of change what's the  formula and how do we work with it in  terms of doing story problems and act  type problems well  the formula you want to know here is the  percent of change is equal to the new  amount minus the old amount divided by  the old amount so basically when we do  new minus old that's giving us you know  what is it changing by how much is the  difference what is it how much has it  changed by but you want to compare it to  what it was originally okay the old  amount  so sometimes students get this little  bit messed up you know they'll divide by  the original amount i'm sorry not the  original amount but the new amount and  you really want to divide about by the  original amount or the old amount  because you're trying to see how it's  changing compared to what it was so  let's go through an example and see how  we work with this so see if you can do  this on your own and we'll go through it  together if a sweater normally sells for  forty dollars  but online it sells for twenty eight  dollars what is the percent change from  the store to the online price right and  we've got some options a b c d  okay so what we would do here is we take  the new price which is 28  minus the old price which is 40 divided  by the  old price which is 40 right so 28 minus  40 is  negative 12  divided by 40. so let's see we'll go to  the calculator here we've got a negative  12 divided by 40 which is  negative 0.3 now you're probably  wondering  what is the negative 4 well the negative  just means that it's going down in price  it's decreasing if it was positive it  means it would be going up in price  this is a decimal to convert it to a  percentage you just move that decimal  two places to the right so this is  actually going to be a 30 change and the  best answer here is going to be letter c  so again new minus old divided by old  that's how you calculate percent of  change
 

